  "title": "Balance cancels the feedback in loops of any length",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "In a loop of r >= 2 rule-60 rings of s cells, contact c_i at cell k_i of R_i and cell 0 of R_(i+1), 2 <= k_i <= s - 2, each contact reading the ends of the two chains arriving at it (law 12, 12) or also its partners (6, 6): (1) C = S + KC over GF(2) with K = z(A + cA^-1), A the weighted cyclic shift A_(i,i-1) = a^(f_i), a = z/(1+z), c = a^(s-2); (2) if k_0 + ... + k_(r-1) = rs/2 every diagonal entry of (I - K)^-1 is 1; (3) then, when no law-(6,6) contact c_j has k_j = s - 2 or k_(j+1) = 2, every contact binds both its rings at every lag at every ring size, the antipodal loops (k_i = s/2) for every r and even s; (4) (I - K)^-1 is a finite sum exactly when r is a power of two and the loop is balanced, with K^(r/2) = 0.",
